  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">YUCATAN PENINSULA II</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The northern coast of Yucatan is a key habitat for 80% of the North American population of Blue-winged Teal. It is also used by 38 wintering species of shorebirds, 20 of which are considered priority species under the US Shorebirds Conservation Plan, such as the Snowy Plover and Wilson&apos;s Plover. Some of the other migratory species that benefit include: American Wigeon, Redhead, Black Skimmer, Black-crowned Night-Heron, Gull-billed Tern, Lesser Scaup, Limpkin, Mangrove Cuckoo, Red Knot, Reddish Egret, Roseate Spoonbill, and Sandwich Tern. The region also hosts the only continental population of American Flamingo. This project will enhance 2,749 acres of coastal wetlands, including mangrove forest. In phase I, hydrological flows were enhanced in an adjacent area, salinity reduced, and soils conditioned to enable reestablishment of mangrove seedlings. This second phase will connect a new network of channels to the channels opened during first phase, multiplying the impact of the previous enhancement and creating larger, interconnected area.</narrative>
  </description>
